    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "bucked up protein powder",
      "buck feed protein",
      "bucked up whey protein",
      "is bucked up protein third party tested",
      "bucked up protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Bucked Up Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For pre-workout fans wondering whether the brand's whey is worth buying: what is in Buck Feed, what Bucked Up says about testing, what it costs per 25 g, and why you will not find the tub on Amazon.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-is">What Is Buck Feed?</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It?</a>
      <a href="#deer-antler">Deer Antler Velvet</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">Bucked Up vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Bucked Up protein powder, sold as Buck Feed, is a sucralose-sweetened whey isolate and concentrate blend with 25 g of protein and 120 calories per scoop, per the label at the time of writing. Bucked Up itself says its products are not third-party tested by NSF or Informed Choice, and the price swings from about $1.79 to $2.67 per 25 g by retailer. Below we check the label, the deer antler, the cost, buyer feedback and the alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Bucked Up Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bucked Up protein powder is an ordinary, well-flavored whey with an unusual add-in and no testing. Buck Feed gives 25 g of protein in 120 calories per scoop, per the label at the time of writing, from a whey isolate and concentrate blend sweetened with sucralose, plus deer antler velvet powder. Bucked Up says its products are not third-party tested by NSF or Informed Choice. The price is the wild card: about $1.79 per 25 g at the cheapest retailer we checked, and $2.67 at the most expensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you already like Bucked Up's pre-workout flavors, you find Buck Feed on sale, and you do not compete in a tested sport. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you need a certified product, avoid sucralose, or want the most protein for your money. Those buyers will do better with the picks in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Buck Feed by the Numbers (Chocolate Label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 33.4 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.79</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Lowest cost per 25 g we saw (Vitacost)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Third-party certifications, per Bucked Up</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Buck Feed, Bucked Up's Protein Soda and the Wheys That Compete With Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first card is the Buck Feed tub itself. Bucked Up does not sell it on Amazon, so that card links to a search rather than a listing. The protein soda is the one Bucked Up protein product sold on Amazon by DAS Labs. The last three are the closest Amazon alternatives, with the prices we saw on November 20, 2026 and the cost per 25 g of protein at those prices.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Bucked Up Protein Powder, and Who Makes It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bucked Up protein powder is Buck Feed, a whey protein made by DAS Labs, the company behind the Bucked Up pre-workout line. Bucked Up built its name on stimulant pre-workouts such as Bucked Up, Woke AF and LFG, and Buck Feed is its post-workout partner product. The label calls it "the newest innovation from Bucked Up, the next step in post workout and training nutrition."
          </p>

          <p>
            The version sold today in most stores is <strong>Buck Feed Original Protein</strong>. The supplement facts we used come from the labels Bucked Up filed with the NIH Dietary Supplement Label Database, entered in 2025, for Chocolate, Vanilla, Chocolate Mint, Horchata and Swole Whip, and they match what Vitacost and The Vitamin Shoppe show on their product pages. Retailers also list Cake Pop and Snickerdoodle, though several flavors were marked sold out when we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some retailers still list a <strong>Buck Feed All-Natural</strong> tub made with grass-fed whey isolate and concentrate. We could not find a current supplement facts panel for it, and Bucked Up's own site blocked our requests, so this review covers the Original formula. If you see the All-Natural version on a shelf, read its label rather than assuming it matches.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is worth separating Buck Feed from Bucked Up's other protein product, the <strong>Lightly Carbonated Protein Soda</strong>. The soda is a canned drink with 25 g of whey isolate per 16 oz can, per its Amazon listing, and it is the only Bucked Up protein product DAS Labs sells on Amazon. If you searched for Bucked Up protein on Amazon and found only cans, that is why.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bucked Up is not the only pre-workout brand to move into protein. Ghost, C4 and Ryse all did the same, and their wheys share a playbook: bold dessert flavors, sucralose and a brand-loyal customer. Our <a href="/c4-protein-powder-review/">C4 protein powder review</a> and <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost protein powder review</a> cover two of those rivals in the same depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Bucked Up Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. Bucked Up states plainly that its products "are not currently third-party tested by organizations such as NSF or Informed Choice." That line comes from the brand's own <a href="https://cms.buckedup.com/third-party-supplement-testing/" rel="nofollow noopener">third-party testing page</a>, and it covers Buck Feed along with the rest of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            What Bucked Up offers instead is manufacturing in "USA GMP-certified facilities," which the same page calls "a baseline level of quality assurance." The Buck Feed label repeats this with a "GMP Good Manufacturing Practice Consistent Quality" seal. GMP is a real standard, but it is about how a factory runs: cleanliness, records and procedures. It does not mean an outside lab tested a finished tub of Buck Feed for banned substances, heavy metals or label accuracy.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also searched the <a href="https://www.nsfsport.com/certified-products/" rel="nofollow noopener">NSF Certified for Sport database</a>. It lists one Bucked Up product, Essentials Creatine Monohydrate, and no Buck Feed protein, so the creatine certification does not carry over to the whey. Some third-party shopping sites show testing badges next to Bucked Up products, but one of them notes that product-specific testing is not verified, so we do not count those.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHY IT MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tested athletes should not use Buck Feed</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you compete in a sport that drug tests (NCAA, pro leagues, powerlifting federations, the military), use a powder with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport or Choice certification. Buck Feed has none, and its deer antler velvet adds a separate risk covered below.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the lack of testing is a trust question rather than a red flag. Plenty of popular wheys are uncertified. But if certification is a deciding factor, <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize ISO100</a> is Informed Choice certified per Dymatize, costs about $2.00 per 25 g in the 5 lb tub on Amazon, and is the cleanest swap here. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide lists more certified options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Bucked Up Buck Feed Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buck Feed contains a whey isolate and concentrate blend, natural flavors, Himalayan rock salt, sucralose and deer antler velvet powder, per the label at the time of writing. The Chocolate flavor adds cocoa powder. There are no gums, no maltodextrin, no oils and no artificial colors, which makes it one of the shorter ingredient lists among gym-brand wheys.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every flavor gives 25 g of protein and 120 calories, but the scoop size and the small numbers move a little by flavor. Chocolate has the biggest scoop at 33.4 g, with 4 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar (1 g added), 1.5 g of fat and 220 mg of sodium. Vanilla uses a 31.3 g scoop with 2 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of sugar (none added), 1 g of fat and 170 mg of sodium.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop (label)</th>
