          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The sweetener and the marketing</h3>

          <p>
            The one thing the front of the tub does not make obvious is the sweetener. Every Buck Feed flavor we checked lists sucralose, an artificial sweetener, yet some retail copy and the brand's "all natural" marketing suggest otherwise. The label's actual claims are narrower: hormone-free, non-GMO, soy free, no artificial colors and, for some flavors, no added sugar. If you avoid sucralose, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buck Feed contains milk, as every whey does. The concentrate part of the blend carries some lactose, so people with strong lactose intolerance may do better with a pure isolate or one of the picks in our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> guide. If you have a milk allergy, kidney disease or are pregnant, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein powder.
          </p>
