        <div id="deer-antler" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does Buck Feed Contain Deer Antler Velvet, and Does It Matter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. The current Buck Feed labels in the NIH database list deer antler velvet powder, and the label copy promises "100mg of deer antler velvet extract" per scoop. It is the most distinctive thing about the formula, and it ties into the Bucked Up brand name and its buck logo.
          </p>

          <p>
            The label does not say what the deer antler velvet is meant to do, and the evidence for performance benefits in people is thin. Deer antler velvet is promoted because it naturally contains growth factors, but 100 mg in a protein shake is a small amount, and we found no good evidence that a dose like this improves muscle gain or recovery beyond the 25 g of whey it comes with. Treat it as a branding feature, not a reason to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tested athletes it is a reason to avoid the product. The US Anti-Doping Agency has warned that deer antler velvet products may contain IGF-1, a growth factor on the World Anti-Doping Agency prohibited list. Combined with Bucked Up's statement that it does not use third-party testing, that makes Buck Feed a poor fit for anyone who could be drug tested.
          </p>

          <p>
            One oddity is worth knowing. Some retailer listings for Chocolate Buck Feed print an ingredient list without the deer antler, while the label Bucked Up filed in 2025 includes it. Formulas can change between batches, so if the ingredient matters to you either way, check the panel on the tub in your hand. A few buyers on The Vitamin Shoppe also noticed and commented on the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

