        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Bucked Up Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buck Feed suits Bucked Up fans who want a well-flavored, simple whey and can buy it on sale. It does not suit tested athletes, sucralose avoiders or buyers chasing the lowest cost per gram.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy Buck Feed if</h3>

          <p>
            You already drink Bucked Up pre-workout and like its flavors. You want 25 g of protein in 120 calories with no gums, fillers or artificial colors. You can find the tub for around $55 or less, which puts it under $1.85 per 25 g. And you are comfortable with an untested product made in a GMP facility, which describes most protein powders on the market.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skip Buck Feed if</h3>

          <p>
            You compete in a drug-tested sport, because Bucked Up says it is not third-party tested and the deer antler velvet adds a separate doping question. You avoid artificial sweeteners, since every flavor uses sucralose. You are very lactose sensitive, since the blend includes concentrate. Or you mainly shop on Amazon, where the tub is not sold.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most of those readers the swap is simple. Tested athletes should look at Dymatize ISO100 or the certified picks in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide. Amazon shoppers who want the same style should try Ghost Whey or Ryse Loaded. Budget buyers should compare everything in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings by cost per 25 g. If you are older or managing a medical condition, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian about how much protein you need before you pick any powder.
          </p>
