        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the GMP seal means third-party testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The GMP seal on the Buck Feed tub describes how the factory runs, not a test of the powder inside. Bucked Up itself says its products are not tested by NSF or Informed Choice. If you need proof that a tub is free of banned substances, look for a named certification, not a manufacturing badge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the all-natural marketing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bucked Up markets Buck Feed with natural, clean language, but every flavor we checked lists sucralose, an artificial sweetener. The label's real claims are narrower: no artificial colors, non-GMO, soy free and hormone-free. Read the ingredient list, not the banner, if you are trying to avoid artificial sweeteners.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full price at one store</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buck Feed ranged from $53.57 to $79.99 for the same 30-serving tub across the retailers we checked, a gap of about 88 cents per 25 g of protein. Over a year of daily shakes that is hundreds of dollars. Compare at least two retailers and wait for a sale before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking it as a tested athlete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Deer antler velvet is the problem here. USADA has warned that these products may contain IGF-1, which is banned in tested sport, and Bucked Up does no third-party testing to rule it out. College, pro and military athletes should choose an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport product instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Buck Feed from unofficial Amazon sellers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bucked Up does not sell Buck Feed tubs on Amazon; only the canned Protein Soda is listed by DAS Labs. A tub from a third-party marketplace seller may be old, stored badly or not genuine. Buy from the brand site or an established retailer, or choose an alternative sold by its own maker on Amazon.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
