        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Bucked Up protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Bucked Up says its products are not currently third-party tested by organizations such as NSF or Informed Choice. It relies on manufacturing in USA GMP-certified facilities instead. The NSF Certified for Sport database lists Bucked Up's Essentials Creatine, but no Buck Feed protein. If you need certified protein for a drug-tested sport, choose an option like Dymatize ISO100, which Dymatize says is Informed Choice certified.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Bucked Up protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a reasonably clean whey by label. Buck Feed has 25 g of protein, 120 calories and 1 to 2 g of sugar per scoop, with no gums, fillers or artificial colors. The caveats are sucralose in every flavor, deer antler velvet with little evidence behind it, and no third-party testing for contaminants. People with kidney disease or other conditions should ask a doctor first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Bucked Up protein contain artificial sweeteners or lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes to both. Every Buck Feed flavor we checked lists sucralose, despite natural-sounding marketing. The protein is a blend of whey isolate and whey concentrate, and the concentrate carries some lactose, so very lactose-sensitive people may notice it. Buck Feed contains milk and is labeled soy free. For a lactose-light option, choose a pure whey isolate instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Bucked Up protein flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers rate Chocolate highly and it is the easiest flavor to find in stock. Fans of the pre-workout often pick Swole Whip, a pineapple, mango and vanilla blend. Vanilla is the most versatile for smoothies and baking and has no added sugar per its label. Seasonal flavors like Horchata and Cake Pop rotate. This is buyer feedback; we did not taste them.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about Bucked Up protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Price is the biggest complaint. Vitamin Shoppe reviewers say the tub roughly doubled from about $40 to $80, and some switched brands. Others find it too sweet, and several flavors are often sold out. Very few buyers complain about mixing or digestion; most describe a creamy, non-chalky shake. Chocolate averaged 4.6 out of 5 from 121 reviews there.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Bucked Up Buck Feed?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Each scoop gives 25 g of protein, per the label at the time of writing, in every flavor we checked. The scoop weighs between about 31 g and 33.4 g depending on flavor, so Buck Feed is roughly 75 to 81% protein by weight. A tub holds 30 servings, so 750 g of protein in total, and calories stay at 120 per scoop.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Bucked Up protein sold on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only the canned Bucked Up Protein Soda is sold on Amazon by DAS Labs, at about $2.66 per 25 g at the price we saw. Buck Feed powder tubs were not listed when we searched in November 2026. Buy the powder from Bucked Up or retailers such as Vitacost and The Vitamin Shoppe, or try Ghost Whey as the closest Amazon match.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

